# gen_fu_pmp1_covergroups_corrigendum.log -- one sentence of gen_fu_pmp1_covergroups.log is wrong
# Produced 2026-09-05T07:16:15Z on soc-l-11. The retained log's bytes are NOT touched; this file
# stands beside it and carries its own manifest row, as a corrigendum must.
#
# THE SENTENCE, at line 100 of gen_fu_pmp1_covergroups.log:
#   "NONE of the four groups has a bin that is unreachable at this configuration"
# It is FALSE as written, and the counter-example is in the same landing's own code.
#
# WHY. CG-PMP-004's cp_first_after_reset was sampled from ONE run-wide flag, set false after ANY PMP CSR
# access, while the plan defines it per CSR: "no prior write to this CSR since reset". cr_reset_read crosses
# it with cp_class, so at most ONE of rst_pmpcfg, rst_pmpaddr, rst_mseccfg and rst_mseccfgh could read yes in
# a run, whichever class the program touched first. The other three were unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION, not by
# configuration, and the sentence above claimed the opposite of a covergroup in the same set.
#
# HOW IT WAS FOUND: the cross-model review of the pmp-step1 range (CM224 MAJOR-2), not by me and not by a
# failing run. The classifier was self-consistent, so nothing failed; only the plan's own definition
# disagreed with it.
#
# WHAT THE SENTENCE SHOULD HAVE SAID: the four groups have no bin unreachable AT THIS CONFIGURATION - which
# is what the PMPEnable, PMPNumRegions and PMPGranularity clauses actually establish - but that is a claim
# about the DUT's parameters, NOT about the samplers. A sampler can make a bin unreachable whatever the
# configuration allows, and this one did. The scope of the claim was wrong, not its evidence.
#
# FIXED: the flag is now per CSR class, so each class's first access reads yes independently.
# The fix and its evidence are in gen_fu_l44_pmp_step1b.log, landing 44.
